<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=three-field-system">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three-field system</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The three-field system is a crop rotation in which a field bears one set of crops in the first year, another in the second, and lies fallow in the third; it was first used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the Eastern Zhou period. It arose independently in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where it replaced the two-field system, which left half the land fallow, between the 9th and 11th centuries. Legumes such as peas and beans restored nitrogen to the soil, and its introduction coincided with the adoption of the mould-board plough.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>771–256 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Europe (9th–11th centuries)</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/plough">Plough<span>c. 2800 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=three-field-system">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
